                "text": "Let me understand the rule and the verifier.\n\nThe verifier: finds objects (non-background, 4-connected, univalued). For each object, takes corners(patch) = the 4 bounding-box corners (ulcorner, urcorner, llcorner, lrcorner). Then underfill with color 1: fills value 1 at indices that are background (most common color). So it fills bounding-box corners that are currently background with 1.\n\nSo for each connected component of color (non-background), compute its bounding box corners, and if those corner cells are background, set to 1.\n\nThe example: input has an object of 8s at positions (2,5),(2,6),(3,6). Bounding box corners: ul=(2,5), ur=(2,6), ll=(3,5), lr=(3,6). (3,5) is background 0 \u2192 filled with 1. Others already 8. Output matches.\n\nThe natural language says three incomplete squares missing a corner. But the verifier handles any number. I need to create 2 demonstration pairs. I need to be careful: the student sees only input-output pairs. I should create inputs where the rule is clear.\n\nStrategy: create grids with multiple disjoint objects (like L-shaped / corner-missing squares) of various colors, and let the verifier compute outputs.\n\nNote: mostcolor is background 0 (assumed). underfill fills only where grid is bg (0). Good.\n\nCareful: corners of the object's bounding box. If object is an L-shape (3 cells forming a 2x2 minus one corner), the missing corner gets filled with 1.\n\nLet me design pair 1 with three incomplete squares as described, of color 8 presumably. Let me place them well-separated.\n\nActually, to teach well, let me include variety: maybe different colors. The rule fills missing corners with blue (1). Let me make an input with 3 L-shapes of color 8, each 2x2 missing a corner.\n\nLet me build a grid, say 9x9.\n\nObject A: cells (1,1),(1,2),(2,1) \u2192 missing corner (2,2). corners: (1,1),(1,2),(2,2),(2,1). (2,2) bg \u2192 fill 1.\n\nObject B: cells (1,6),(1,7),(2,7) \u2192 missing (2,6). corners (1,6),(1,7),(2,7),(2,6) \u2192 fill (2,6).\n\nObject C: cells (6,1),(7,1),(7,2) \u2192 missing (6,2). corners (6,1),(7,1),(7,2),(6,2) \u2192 fill (6,2).\n\nWait object C: cells (6,1),(7,1),(7,2). Bounding box rows 6-7, cols 1-2. corners: (6,1),(6,2),(7,1),(7,2). Present: (6,1),(7,1),(7,2). Missing (6,2). Good.\n\nMake grid 9x9. Let me verify these objects are separate (not 4-connected). Yes.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input with this input. The environment will compute output.\n\nFor pair 2, vary: different colors maybe, and different missing corners, larger grid. Let's do color 3 objects maybe. But rule says \"blue square\" fill. Most color background 0.\n\nHmm, but the objects function uses univalued=True, so each object is one color. Fine.\n\nLet me think about whether the solver can infer from just 2 pairs. The natural language rule: three incomplete squares missing a corner \u2192 fill missing corners blue. But the verifier fills bounding-box corners with 1 regardless of shape. To be safe, my demos should be consistent: each input has exactly coric shapes missing corners. I'll use exactly the pattern in the example: L-shaped, missing one corner.\n\nActually wait\u2014what if the bounding box is bigger than 2x2? For the demo I'll keep 2x2 L-shapes.\n\nLet me design pair 2 with 3 objects of a different color, say 4, and different orientations, and maybe positions. Also maybe include a case where the missing corner is the upper-left etc.\n\nActually to be robust, let me make one pair have 3 objects as in the teacher example (color 8), and another pair with 3 objects of color 6, in different orientations.\n\nLet me just do it.\n\nPair 1: 9x9 as above.\n\nLet me write it out:\n\nRow0: all 0\nRow1: 0,8,8,0,0,0,8,8,0\nRow2: 0,8,0,0,0,0,0,8,0\nRow3: all 0\nRow4: all 0\nRow5: all 0\nRow6: 0,8,0,0,0,0,0,0,0\nRow7: 0,8,8,0,0,0,0,0,0\nRow8: all 0\n\nWait object A cells: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1). Row1 col1=8,col2=8; row2 col1=8. Missing (2,2). Good.\nObject B: (1,6),(1,7),(2,7). Row1 col6=8,col7=8; row2 col7=8. Missing (2,6). Good.\nObject C: (6,1),(7,1),(7,2). Row6 col1=8; row7 col1=8,col2=8. Missing (6,2). Good.\n\nCheck row widths: Row1: indices 0..8: 0,8,8,0,0,0,8,8,0 \u2192 9 entries. Row2: 0,8,0,0,0,0,0,8,0 \u2192 9. Row6: 0,8,0,0,0,0,0,0,0 \u2192 9. Row7: 0,8,8,0,0,0,0,0,0 \u2192 9.\n\nGood.\n\nPair 2: maybe 10x10 with 3 objects of color 2, different orientations, missing corners. Let's do:\nObject A: (1,1),(2,1),(2,2) missing (1,2) (upper-right).\nObject B: (1,5),(2,5),(2,6) missing (1,6).\nHmm those are same orientation. Let's vary.\n\nObject A: (2,2),(2,3),(3,3) \u2192 bounding box rows2-3 cols2-3, present (2,2),(2,3),(3,3), missing (3,2).\nObject B: (2,7),(3,7),(3,8) \u2192 present (2,7),(3,7),(3,8)? bounding box rows2-3 cols7-8, corners (2,7),(2,8),(3,7),(3,8). present (2,7),(3,7),(3,8), missing (2,8).\nObject C: (6,3),(6,4),(7,3) \u2192 missing (7,4).\n\nColors: use 2.\n\nActually maybe simpler to keep three objects, all same color. The example uses 8. Using a different color tests that fill is always 1.\n\nLet me build a 10x10 grid.\n\nRow0: 0*10\nRow1: 0*10\nRow2: 0,0,2,2,0,0,0,2,0,0\nRow3: 0,0,0,2,0,0,0,2,2,0\nRow4-5: 0\nRow6: 0,0,0,2,2,0,0,0,0,0\nRow7: 0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,0,0\nRow8-9: 0\n\nCheck object A: (2,2),(2,3),(3,3). row2 col2=2,col3=2; row3 col3=2. Missing corner (3,2). Good.\nObject B: (2,7),(3,7),(3,8). row2 col7=2; row3 col7=2,col8=2. Missing (2,8). Good.\nObject C: (6,3),(6,4),(7,3). row6 col3=2,col4=2; row7 col3=2. Missing (7,4). Good.\n\nRow2: 0,0,2,2,0,0,0,2,0,0 \u2192 indices: 0:0,1:0,2:2,3:2,4:0,5:0,6:0,7:2,8:0,9:0. 10 entries.\nRow3: 0,0,0,2,0,0,0,2,2,0 \u2192 10 entries.\nRow6: 0,0,0,2,2,0,0,0,0,0 \u2192 10.\nRow7: 0,0,0,2,0,0,0,0,0,0 \u2192 10.\n\nGood. Objects separated (A cols2-3 rows2-3, B cols7-8 rows2-3, C cols3-4 rows6-7). Yes.\n\nNow call show_transformed_input for both.\n\nWait\u2014the instruction says \"assemble exactly 2 demonstration pairs\". I should call the tool twice. Let me do concurrent calls.